          "text": "# Dabih: A Binary Written in Starlight\n\n*In Capricornus, one modest point of light carries a hidden companion\u2014and a clue to how astronomers read the sky.*\n\nDabih is easy to mistake for a quiet star. Look south on a clear late-summer or autumn evening, find the small, faint outline of Capricornus, and you\u2019ll find it among the constellation\u2019s brightest points. To the eye, it is a single light. In the catalog, however, the object identified as \u03b2\u00b9 Cap is marked as a spectroscopic binary: a system whose paired stars reveal themselves through their changing spectra.\n\nThat distinction is a reminder that the night sky has more than one scale. A star can appear as one point because its companions are too close together, or because their light blends at the eye or telescope. In a spectroscopic binary, astronomers detect the system through the light itself. As the stars orbit, their motion shifts the patterns of dark and bright lines in the spectrum. Those shifts can disclose a companion even when the stars cannot be seen separately.\n\nSIMBAD lists \u03b2\u00b9 Cap\u2019s spectral type as G9II:+B8p:Si:\u2014a classification that suggests a complicated, unusual stellar system rather than a simple sunlike star. Its cataloged visual magnitude is 3.08, making it a naked-eye object under reasonably dark skies. The same record gives a parallax of 8.3966 milliarcseconds. Inverting that measurement puts the system at roughly 119 parsecs, or about 390 light-years away. Those figures are measurements, not a portrait: from Earth, the system still arrives as one small pinprick. ([SIMBAD: HIP 100345](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=HIP%20100345))\n\nThere is a pleasing mismatch between the star\u2019s apparent simplicity and the evidence needed to understand it. The unaided eye gathers all the light into one point; a spectrograph spreads that light into a narrow band, where the stars\u2019 motion leaves its signature. The instrument does not merely magnify the view. It changes what counts as a view.\n\n**How to find it:** Capricornus lies south of Aquila and east of Sagittarius, low compared with the more familiar northern constellations. Dabih\u2019s coordinates are about right ascension 20 hours 21 minutes and declination \u221214\u00b047\u2032. Use those coordinates in a planetarium app or star chart, then look for the constellation on a late-summer or autumn evening. Around September, the region is well placed for evening viewing; from northern locations, Dabih may sit low in the southern sky, so choose a site with an open southern horizon. Its magnitude makes it a practical naked-eye target away from bright city lights, though binoculars can help pick it out among the surrounding stars.\n\nA telescope is not required to appreciate the puzzle\u2014and a sharper view may not split this particular pair into two points. \u201cSpectroscopic binary\u201d describes how the companionship is detected, not a guarantee that two stars will appear side by side in an eyepiece. Instead, let Dabih remain a single bright point. The invisible motion is the story: two stars orbiting, their light carrying a rhythm across the distance.\n\n**Source:** [SIMBAD entry for HIP 100345 / \u03b2\u00b9 Cap](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=HIP%20100345)"
